  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 228 AM EDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nMostly clear skies are overhead early this morning as local \nobservations and satellite imagery hint at spotty fog trying to \nform, mainly in the interior areas. However, given the 'bust' in \nrainfall from yesterday, confidence in the fog forecast is somewhat \nlow for this morning; while I'm sure some patches could become dense \nfor a short while, given that the fog is not the primary concern for \nthe forecast package, will leave the mentions of patchy fog in the \nforecast as is for now; although, if the fog overachieves, an update \nwill be needed into the mid-morning hours. \n\nExpect the warmest temperatures of the week today as highs soar into \nthe 80s to lower 90s across the area, with the highest temperatures \nbeing seen in the interior west. With the anomalously warm air \noverhead, the U.P. is theoretically sitting on prime real-estate for \nsevere weather potential today; convection could become sfc-based, \nprovided there is enough forcing to overcome the small to modest \ncapping at the 850-700mb level. In addition, MUCAPEs could jump to 1 \nto 3+ kJ/kg by this afternoon, with effective bulk shear in the \nballpark of 40 to 60 knots. However, a large potential limiting \nfactor is the forcing mechanism to get convection going. Recently, \nsome of the CAMs, as well as the GFS, are keeping most of the \nconvection either just south of the WI/U.P. border or calling for a \nprecipitation bust altogether. Because of this, decided to lower the \nconvection chances today and even moreso tonight. That being said, \nthe latest (4z) run of the HRRR and RAP has shifted the convection \nthat could develop this afternoon northwards into the U.P.. Should \nwe have convection develop overhead today, there is certainly the \nthreat for severe weather, with all threats on the table. However, \nthe main threat would be for severe/damaging winds, followed by \nisolated cells producing large hail. Therefore, SPC has still kept \nmost of the west and central under a Slight (>15%) Risk, whereas the \neast remains under a Marginal (>5%) Risk; the tornado risk is >2% \nover the west and central, whereas there is a 15% chance for \nconsiderable damaging winds and hail; as for the east, there is only \na 5% chance for marginally damaging winds and hail as the convection \nwould most likely not reach over there until the evening or later \nhours. \n\nAnother round of showers and storms potentially bringing severe \nweather with it is possible on Monday as a Clipper low moves through \nthe Upper Great Lakes. Currently, severe winds are the primary \nthreat, although large hail is a secondary threat as showers and \nstorms associated with the low pass by. Again, looking at the latest \nCAMs, I'm not very confident on either the placement or timing of \nthe convection; it is possible (at least a 50% chance in my own \nestimation) that the convection misses us entirely on Monday, most \nlikely to the south of us (hitting WI instead). However, given the \nknown issue of CAMs struggling to resolve the placement of \nindividual cells, there is certainly still a non-zero chance severe \nweather could be seen in the U.P. come Monday as bulk shears still \nremain elevated at 40 to 55 knots and MUCAPEs could rebound up to \naround 2 kJ/kg near the WI border. Therefore, while I'm leaning \ntowards Monday being a 'bust' for severe convection, I wouldn't rule \nit out just quite yet. \n\nBehind the Clipper low on Monday, expect quieter weather across the \nregion as geopotential heights rise over the central CONUS into \nCanada. With a semi-omega block appearance forming over the central \nCONUS, we could see a few weak Canadian shortwaves move across the \narea (mainly the first weekend of August). However, because recent \nmedium-range runs are showing mid-level heights increasing locally \nover the Upper Midwest and Great Lakes for the middle of the week, \nthinking we will more likely than not remain dry until potentially \nnext weekend.",
